# columns.view: two to four columns, split by vertical bars. No border, no title, no rule.
#
# The way in is a decorator over a plain markdown table, which is the whole point: where
# this hook does not run, the reader still gets a real table.
#
#   @{view:columns}
#   | | |
#   | --- | --- |
#   | Status | all green |
#
# The header row is MANDATORY and its cells may be empty (`| | |`): markdown needs one,
# this view never prints it. An empty label cell continues the row above, so a long entry
# spans several rows under one label.
#
# ONE file draws every width, two, three or four columns, and no conditional is involved:
# a column NO row carries is dropped together with the separator written just before it
# (the hollow rule, src/layout/columns.ts). So the two ends are the anchors, `label` first
# and `content` last WHATEVER the arity, and `mid1` then `mid2` fill in between. The
# header of the table decides, and every row below must hold that same count.
#
# The HEADER row spends the TONE SLOT, so ONE file dresses every kind and no second template
# ever has to exist:
#
#   @{view:columns}                 the default below, teal
#   @{view:columns, type:warning}   yellow, and the message still says what it IS
#   @{view:columns, tone:dim}       grey, a look with no semantics attached
#
# The ink is on the header and NOT on the label column, which is the whole difference with
# lines.view, and it follows the direction the eye reads in: a bar runs down the page, so a
# column is read DOWNWARDS from the word that names it, and that word is its header. Where
# lines.view rules ACROSS, an element is read along its row and what names it is the label
# at the head of that row. Same table, same two roles, and each view inks the axis it built.
#
# The tone is an INDEXED colour on purpose. Every base-sixteen name in this palette is
# written `1;3x` (style.ts), and that weight is not decoration: it promotes the colour to
# its bright slot. So "coloured but not bold" cannot be had by dropping the `1`, which would
# change the hue, and is bought from the index side of the palette instead.
#
# A view only answers a kind if it SPENDS the slot, which is why the head line does. The
# cost, pinned in the suite: a table wanting NO header (`| | |`) has nowhere to carry the
# ink, so `type:` changes nothing at all there.
#
# The bar is the glyph the box draws its sides with, in the same dim grey, and it is the
# only furniture here. Each separator is written as a CLOSED span, `{{dim}}  │  {{/}}`,
# because a hollow column takes the whole run that FOLLOWS it and a closer left behind
# would leak. Every separator here is the same, so which side that rule takes shows on
# box.view alone.
# cap="1/3" keeps a long label from pushing the content to the margin: past a third of the
# width it FOLDS, one row per line, in step with the content beside it. It costs the row
# height and never a character, an ellipsis being a piece of the data the reader cannot
# get back. The content column is the LAST field, emitted verbatim, so it carries whatever
# emphasis the message wrote.
#
# @head draws ONCE above the rows, from the table's own header line, and only when that
# line says something: `| | |` is what markdown forces on a table wanting no header, so an
# all-blank one draws nothing here and the list starts at the first row, exactly as before.
# A header word longer than every value under it still widens its column, because the head
# row joins the MEASUREMENT even when it is not iterated. Its ink is re-opened on EVERY cell
# and that repetition is not idle: the bar's own closer is a full reset, so an ink opened
# once at the head of the line dies on the first separator and every cell after it reads
# plain. An opener is also what a hollow column can take away safely, having no closer
# to leave orphaned behind it.
#
# The fenced form works too, for a message with no table to give: a `rows:` list whose
# every item is `- <label> <the rest of the line>`. There every declared field is assigned,
# so nothing is hollow and all four columns draw.
@tone teal
@fields rows label mid1 mid2 content
@each rows cap="1/3"
@head {{b}}{{tone}}${label}{{/}}{{dim}}  │  {{/}}{{b}}{{tone}}${mid1}{{/}}{{dim}}  │  {{/}}{{b}}{{tone}}${mid2}{{/}}{{dim}}  │  {{/}}{{b}}{{tone}}${content}{{/}}
${label}{{dim}}  │  {{/}}${mid1}{{dim}}  │  {{/}}${mid2}{{dim}}  │  {{/}}${content}
@end
